.TH JPACKER 8 "June 2026" "jpacker 1.3.1" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
jpacker \- A robust Pacman wrapper and AUR helper with source-based build management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jpacker
.I <operation>
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ targets... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jpacker
is a C++20 based AUR helper designed to act as a seamless wrapper for
.BR pacman (8).
It extends pacman's functionality by handling AUR packages, providing one-off source builds for official packages, and managing persistent source-based build configurations (similar to Gentoo's package.env).

Any operation or flag not explicitly handled by jpacker is passed through to pacman directly.

.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.B \-S, \-Syu, \-Syy
Install packages or update the system. If a target is found in the official repositories, pacman is used. If not, it is searched in the AUR.
If a package is marked for source-build (via \fBadd-src\fR), it will be built from source even if it exists in the official repositories.
.TP
.B \-Ss \fI<query>\fR
Search for packages in both the official repositories and the AUR.
.TP
.B \-R, \-Rs, \-Rns \fI<target>\fR
Remove packages. This is a direct wrapper around \fBpacman -R\fR.
.TP
.B build \fI<package> [VAR=VALUE...]\fR
Perform a one-off build from source for a package (either from Official Repos or AUR).
Environment variables (like CFLAGS) can be passed directly.
.br
Example: \fIjpacker build coreutils CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native"\fR
.TP
.B deps [\-\-recursive] \fI<package>\fR
Inspect an AUR package's runtime, make, and check dependencies, then classify them as official repository dependencies, AUR dependencies, provided dependencies, or unknown dependencies.
With \fB\-\-recursive\fR, recursively inspect AUR dependencies. Official repository, provided, and unknown dependencies are shown but not traversed.
This command does not clone, build, install dependencies, or calculate build order.
.TP
.B plan \fI<package>\fR
Display a build order plan for an AUR package and its recursive AUR dependencies.
Official repository and repository-provided dependencies are not included in the build plan. Unknown dependencies are reported as unresolved.
This command does not clone, build, or install dependencies.
.TP
.B fetch \fI<package>\fR
Safely retrieve the AUR build repositories needed to inspect an AUR package and its recursive AUR dependencies before any build/install stage.
Fetch targets are resolved by PackageBase. Official repository and provided dependencies are not cloned. Unknown dependencies are reported as unresolved.
Missing AUR repositories are cloned. Existing cloned repositories run only \fBgit fetch origin\fR.
This command does not update the working tree and does not run \fBgit pull\fR, merge, reset, build, or install operations.
Future behavior that advances a working tree is not implemented by \fBfetch\fR and should be considered in a separate issue as a command or option such as \fBsync\fR, \fBupdate\fR, or \fBfetch --update\fR.
.TP
.B upgrade
Perform a system upgrade. This first runs \fBsudo pacman -Syu\fR, then checks packages marked via \fBadd-src\fR and rebuilds them from source only when their PKGBUILD version is newer than the installed package.
During source package updates, jpacker can show a git diff if changes are detected.
.TP
.B clean
Clean package caches. This first runs \fBsudo pacman -Sc\fR to clean the official package cache, and then prompts to remove build artifacts from the jpacker cache directory (\fI~/.cache/jpacker/\fR). Log files are preserved.
.TP
.B add-src \fI<package> [VAR=VALUE...]\fR
Mark a package to always be built from source. This creates a configuration file in \fI/etc/jpacker/package.build/\fR.
You can optionally append environment variables to the configuration.
.TP
.B del-src \fI<package>\fR
Unmark a package from source-build management. Removes the configuration file.
.TP
.B revert \fI<package>\fR
Unmark a package AND immediately reinstall the official binary version (via \fBpacman -S\fR) if the package exists in the official repositories. Use this to restore a package to its standard state.
.TP
.B edit-src \fI<package>\fR
Open the build configuration file for the specified package using the defined \fBEDITOR\fR.
.TP
.B list-src
List all packages currently registered for source-based builds along with their custom environment variables.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-noedit
Skip the interactive review/edit of PKGBUILD files before building.
.TP
.BR \-\-nodiff
Skip the interactive git diff check during package updates.
.TP
.BR \-h , \ \-\-help
Display the help message and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The main configuration file is located at \fI/etc/jpacker/jpacker.conf\fR.
It supports the following keys:
.TP
.B NOEDIT
Set to \fBtrue\fR to disable PKGBUILD review prompts globally.
.TP
.B NODIFF
Set to \fBtrue\fR to disable git diff checks during updates globally.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Specify the preferred text editor. If the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable is set, it takes precedence.
Editor command tokens are restricted to simple command words and options for safety.
.TP
.B LOGFILE
Specify the path to the log file. Supports tilde expansion (e.g., \fB~/logs/jpacker.log\fR).
Default is \fI~/.cache/jpacker/jpacker.log\fR.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/jpacker/jpacker.conf
Global configuration file. Controls editor settings, log locations, and review prompts.
.TP
.I /etc/jpacker/package.build/
Directory containing configuration files for packages marked with \fBadd-src\fR.
Each file corresponds to a package name and can define environment variables (e.g., CFLAGS) that are applied during \fBmakepkg\fR execution.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jpacker/
Default directory used for downloading and building packages (respects \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jpacker/jpacker.log
Default log file location.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
The editor used for reviewing PKGBUILDs and editing configurations.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Used to determine the build directory location.

.SH EXAMPLES
.B Update system and source-marked packages:
.RS
jpacker upgrade
.RE

.B Install a package from AUR without review:
.RS
jpacker -S google-chrome --noedit
.RE

.B Classify dependencies of an AUR package:
.RS
jpacker deps google-chrome
.RE

.B Recursively inspect AUR dependencies:
.RS
jpacker deps --recursive google-chrome
.RE

.B Show a build order plan for an AUR package:
.RS
jpacker plan mongodb-bin
.RE

.B Safely clone or fetch AUR build repositories:
.RS
jpacker fetch mongodb-bin
.RE

.B Mark a package to use custom optimizations:
.RS
jpacker add-src neofetch CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native"
.RE

.B Clean caches to free up space:
.RS
jpacker clean
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacman (8),
.BR makepkg (8)

